{"entries":{"chapter-34-study-hearing-loss-assessment-and-management|Chapter 34 Study: Hearing Loss: Assessment and Management":["u1"],"summary|Summary":["u2"],"copy|Presbycusis is heterogeneous age-related peripheral and central auditory dysfunction; it usually begins at high frequencies and progresses into the speech range (p489–490).":["u3"],"copy|Significant loss affects approximately 44% in their sixties, 66% at 70–79, and 90% after 80 in the chapter's cited estimates (p490).":["u4"],"copy|Genetic susceptibility interacts with noise, ototoxic drugs, smoking and comorbidity; diabetes and vascular disease can promote cochlear injury (p489–491).":["u5"],"copy|Ask about speech understanding, especially in noise, rather than only whether sounds are audible. A hearing-handicap questionnaire can identify everyday effects (p489, p493, p500).":["u6"],"copy|Pure-tone audiometry, tympanometry, acoustic reflexes and word-recognition testing characterize different aspects of impairment (p489).":["u7"],"copy|One cited professional definition uses threshold loss of at least 25 dB at any frequency between 250 and 8000 Hz; functional impact also depends on frequency pattern (p493).":["u8"],"copy|Sensorineural loss often begins with high-frequency hair-cell damage; a flat audiogram can reflect metabolic/supporting-structure dysfunction (p495).":["u9"],"copy|Tailor digital hearing aids to the audiogram; they can improve hearing and speech discrimination but do not regenerate lost hair cells (p499).":["u10"],"copy|Address acceptance and continued use, not just device provision; many eligible older people never try an aid or stop using it (p499).":["u11"],"copy|When aids cannot provide sufficient correction, consider cochlear implantation. The chapter cites benefit when sentence scores in the poorer ear are below 60% (p499).":["u12"],"copy|Hearing aids amplify sound using remaining sensory cells; implants bypass those cells to stimulate the auditory nerve electrically (p499).":["u13"],"copy|Reduce noise, ototoxin and smoking exposure; exercise and healthy diet may help, but the degree to which specific interventions delay presbycusis remains uncertain (p498).":["u14"],"copy|Antioxidant results are inconsistent; a small low-frequency benefit from folate in a Dutch study cannot be generalized to restoration of high-frequency hearing (p499).":["u15"],"copy|“I hear but cannot understand” may reflect central auditory dysfunction that amplification alone cannot resolve (p499–500).":["u16"],"exam-questions|Exam questions":["u17"],"copy|2025-Jun Q26":["u18"],"copy|CHAPTER 34s / HAZZARD":["u19"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 34 - Hearing Loss: Assessment and Management; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u20"],"copy|Excluded from the IMA required reading list (P005-2026); cited once in past papers.":["u21"],"copy|Definition & epidemiology":["u22"],"copy|Assessment":["u23"],"copy|Management":["u24"],"copy|Prevention, drugs & exam traps":["u25"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (1):":["u26"],"copy|P005-2026 excludes Chapter 34 and separately assigns the NEJM review on age-related hearing loss.":["u21"],"copy|Past-exam questions linked to this chapter (3):":["u26"],"copy|Key Clinical Point 2 gives over 55% (p489): it describes the proportion of variation in the study population attributable to genetic differences, and cannot count how many individual cases are inherited.":["u40"],"copy|Both B and D received credit in September 2024; a clinic screen alone cannot confirm the type or degree of loss (p493).":["u49"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Sudden unilateral hearing loss warrants urgent assessment.":["u48"],"copy|Lin, NEJM 2024, pp1506–1507.":["u103"],"copy|Table 34-2 places /sh/ in שלום (shalom) at 1000–2000 Hz, whereas /f/, /k/, /s/, /t/ and /th/ are 3000–8000 Hz (p494).":["u53"],"copy|Hazzard pp499–500.":["u104"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): Lin describes PTA4 generally at least 60 dB in one or both ears with insufficient functional benefit from hearing aids as a reason to consider implant referral.":["u105"],"copy|NEJM, 2024, pp1509–1511.":["u68"],"copy|The chapter describes observational data (p500).":["u81"],"copy|Current practice (not the book): The later ACHIEVE trial found no difference in three-year cognitive change across its full cohort, with slower decline in a prespecified comparison of higher-risk participants.":["u82"],"copy|Offer rehabilitation for communication and participation; the evidence does not promise prevention of dementia for each patient (ACHIEVE trial, 2023; Lin, NEJM 2024, p1511).":["u83"],"copy|CHAPTER 34 / HAZZARD":["u19"],"what-the-exams-asked|What the exams asked":["u27"],"copy|When poor speech understanding despite well-fitted hearing aids warrants a cochlear implant evaluation — June 2026 Q39. The item cites an article. P005-2026 assigns the NEJM review separately from the excluded textbook chapter.":["u30"],"family-history-and-assessment|Family history and assessment":["u31"],"recognizing-speech-in-noise|Recognizing speech in noise":["u32"],"hearing-aids-and-referral-for-an-implant|Hearing aids and referral for an implant":["u33"],"inner-ear-brain-and-cognition|Inner ear, brain and cognition":["u34"],"prevention-and-emerging-treatments|Prevention and emerging treatments":["u35"],"israeli-context|Israeli context":["u36"],"reading-an-audiogram-alongside-the-history|Reading an audiogram alongside the history":["u362"],"from-a-pressure-wave-to-a-conversation|From a pressure wave to a conversation":["u363"],"read-sound-level-and-frequency-separately|Read sound level and frequency separately":["u364"],"choosing-tests-for-the-complaint|Choosing tests for the complaint":["u365"],"risks-models-and-the-limits-of-prevention-evidence|Risks, models and the limits of prevention evidence":["u366"],"evaluate-benefit-from-amplification-then-the-need-for-a-different-device|Evaluate benefit from amplification, then the need for a different device":["u367"],"exam-traps|Exam traps":["u368"],"copy|A preserved low-frequency threshold can coexist with disabling high-frequency and speech-in-noise loss (p494–497).":["u369"],"copy|A 4-kHz unilateral notch calls for an exposure history; it is not the bilateral, diffuse aging pattern in the adjacent panel (p496).":["u370"],"copy|Aided sentence recognition is measured in percent correct, while PTA4 is a pure-tone average in decibels. Do not substitute one referral measure for the other (p499–500; separately assigned NEJM review).":["u371"],"copy|Population heritability describes variation among people; it does not assign an individual's hearing loss to a single cause (p489–491).":["u372"],"copy|Increased speaking volume alone may leave high-frequency consonants indistinct; reduce competing sound and allow time for processing (p494–497).":["u373"],"copy|The book's implant discussion uses a poorer-ear sentence-recognition percentage. A pure-tone threshold is expressed in decibels (p495, p499).":["u374"],"past-exam-questions|Past exam questions":["u375"],"recall-drill|Recall drill":["u379"],"copy|Study summary of Chapter 34; the book and the full chapter are the reference.":["u380"],"copy|The Framingham comparison found that blood relatives had more similar hearing thresholds than spouses.":["u39"],"copy|A patient can inherit susceptibility and also sustain cochlear injury from diabetes, vascular disease, noise or ototoxic medication.":["u41"],"copy|Men generally develop deficits earlier, and sex differences narrow later in life (pp489–491).":["u42"],"copy|Ask whether conversation becomes unintelligible in company and whether the patient avoids social situations.":["u43"],"copy|The short Hearing Handicap Inventory for the Elderly measures perceived functional difficulty.":["u44"],"copy|A typical gradual bilateral history supports a presumptive clinical diagnosis after checking for other causes.":["u45"],"copy|A bedside whisper test has variable sensitivity.":["u46"],"copy|Otoscopy can reveal obstructing wax, and audiometry measures each ear’s thresholds and helps distinguish sensorineural and conductive causes.":["u47"],"copy|A voice can be loud enough to hear while its words are indistinct.":["u50"],"copy|Reduced sensitivity at high frequencies obscures consonants that distinguish one word from another.":["u51"],"copy|In June 2025, ספר (sefer) contains both /s/ and /f/.":["u52"],"copy|Vowels and context can carry a quiet conversation until competing voices mask those remaining cues (pp494–495, Table 34-2).":["u54"],"copy|The audiogram in Figure 34-5A slopes downward in both ears as pitch rises.":["u55"],"copy|In panel B a flatter pattern spans low and high frequencies; panel C shows a unilateral notch typical of noise exposure.":["u56"],"copy|At the same pure-tone threshold, two people can have different speech-in-noise performance.":["u57"],"copy|The QuickSIN test measures the signal advantage a listener needs over competing noise.":["u58"],"copy|Slower delivery and a quieter room can immediately improve communication (pp495–497).":["u59"],"copy|June 2026 Q39.":["u60"],"copy|PTA4 averages thresholds at 500, 1000, 2000 and 4000 Hz in one ear.":["u61"],"copy|A better-ear average of 70 dB falls in the severe range.":["u62"],"copy|Speech remains hard to understand even in quiet with properly fitted aids.":["u63"],"copy|Refer to a cochlear implant centre for aided speech testing and an individual assessment.":["u64"],"copy|Hazzard discusses a different measure, sentence recognition below 60% in the poorer ear.":["u66"],"copy|Decibels quantify the sound level needed to detect tones; percent correct measures speech recognition.":["u67"],"copy|A digital hearing aid supplies frequency-specific acoustic gain to a functioning cochlea.":["u69"],"copy|Figure 34-6 shows improved thresholds through about 3000 Hz in one listener, with limited recovery at the highest frequencies.":["u70"],"copy|A cochlear implant encodes sound as electrical stimulation of the auditory nerve.":["u71"],"copy|Understanding speech after implantation takes practice; benefit varies with neural function and other factors, and chronological age alone does not rule out benefit (pp499–500).":["u72"],"copy|The basal cochlea encodes high frequencies and the apex low frequencies.":["u73"],"copy|Outer hair cells amplify and sharpen mechanical responses; inner hair cells convert movement into signals for auditory nerve fibres.":["u74"],"copy|The stria vascularis maintains the ionic gradient that powers transduction.":["u75"],"copy|Damage to basal hair cells can produce a sloping audiogram, whereas loss of this gradient can raise thresholds across frequencies.":["u76"],"copy|Synaptic or neural loss can impair discrimination even with relatively preserved tone detection.":["u77"],"copy|Several lesions can coexist (pp492–497, Figures 34-1 to 34-5).":["u78"],"copy|Hearing loss is associated with faster cognitive decline.":["u79"],"copy|Listening effort, social isolation and common pathology are plausible explanations.":["u80"],"copy|Permanent hair-cell loss limits recovery.":["u84"],"copy|In mice, apparently transient noise damage can leave lasting synaptic injury, and different strains lose hearing at different ages.":["u85"],"copy|Oxidative stress, mitochondrial injury and vascular changes are candidate mechanisms.":["u86"],"copy|Their importance differs between animal models and human ageing.":["u87"],"copy|Reducing avoidable noise and ototoxic exposures is practical; vitamin supplementation and hair-cell regeneration have no established role in routine treatment of presbycusis.":["u88"],"copy|Experimental nerve or brainstem stimulation and regeneration are discussed separately from everyday rehabilitation (pp491, 497–501).":["u89"],"copy|בירור ושיקום.":["u90"],"copy|קושי לעקוב אחר כמה דוברים, בקשות חוזרות לחזור על מילים או הגברת הטלוויזיה מצדיקים פנייה לרופא המשפחה.":["u91"],"copy|רופא אף־אוזן־גרון יכול לזהות ולטפל בשעווה חוסמת ולהפנות למכון שמיעה.":["u92"],"copy|קלינאי תקשורת בודק בכל אוזן את סוג הירידה וחומרתה ומתאים אמצעי שיקום לפי התוצאות.":["u93"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, ירידה בשמיעה: אבחון ושיקום, ״שלבים באבחון ירידה בשמיעה״, שלבים 1–4.":["u94"],"copy|מימון והתנסות.":["u95"],"copy|לפי דף משרד הבריאות מ־24.11.2026, הקופה משתתפת במימון מכשירים מגיל 18.":["u96"],"copy|לאחר בדיקת אף־אוזן־גרון ובדיקת שמיעה במכון שבהסדר, תהליך ההתאמה מתחיל בתוך 60 יום מבדיקת הרופא.":["u97"],"copy|אפשר להתנסות במכשיר לפחות חודש.":["u98"],"copy|הזכאות הרגילה מתחדשת אחת לשלוש וחצי שנים.":["u99"],"copy|שינוי משמעותי בשמיעה שמצריך החלפה מאפשר השתתפות גם קודם לכן.":["u100"],"copy|ברכישה פרטית יש לברר מראש אם הקופה מציעה החזר.":["u101"],"copy|משרד הבריאות, מימון מכשירי שמיעה, ״שלבי התאמת מכשירי שמיעה״, ״התנסות במכשיר לפני הקנייה״ ו״החלפת מכשיר שמיעה״.":["u102"],"copy|The horizontal axis moves from low to high frequencies.":["u190"],"copy|The vertical axis runs downward as more intense sound is needed for detection.":["u191"],"copy|In the book's bilateral sloping example, low-frequency tones remain audible and higher-frequency thresholds are poor.":["u192"],"copy|A person can follow a voice in a quiet room yet miss the consonants that distinguish similar words in a group.":["u193"],"copy|A flat loss suggests a different pattern; a unilateral notch calls for an exposure history rather than attribution to age alone (p494–495).":["u194"],"copy|Pure-tone detection and word recognition answer different questions.":["u195"],"copy|Tympanometry and acoustic reflexes assess middle-ear function.":["u196"],"copy|Speech-in-noise assessment challenges auditory and cognitive processing under competing signals.":["u197"],"copy|Ask what listening situations the person wants to recover before choosing and fitting a device; then assess how it works in those situations (p493–497, p499–500).":["u198"],"copy|Figures 34-1 through 34-3 follow sound from the cochlear fluid to the auditory nerve.":["u199"],"copy|The eardrum and ossicles transmit vibration to the cochlea, producing a traveling wave and motion of the basilar membrane.":["u200"],"copy|In Figure 34-1, scala vestibuli and scala tympani contain perilymph; scala media contains endolymph.":["u201"],"copy|The stria vascularis and spiral ligament along the lateral wall maintain the ionic conditions that allow sound to be converted into a signal.":["u202"],"copy|The figure describes an endolymphatic potential of about +70 to +100 mV.":["u203"],"copy|A vascular or metabolic injury may therefore impair auditory sensitivity even when surviving hair cells still look relatively intact (p491–493).":["u204"],"copy|Figure 34-2 distinguishes the one row of inner hair cells from the three rows of outer hair cells.":["u205"],"copy|Inner hair cells perform most of the sensory transduction communicated to afferent spiral ganglion neurons.":["u206"],"copy|Outer hair cells amplify the mechanical response and receive predominantly efferent innervation.":["u207"],"copy|In the surface view in Figure 34-3 the inner bundles are linear, whereas the outer bundles form a W-like pattern.":["u208"],"copy|Relative motion of basilar and tectorial membranes bends the stereocilia, opens transduction channels and allows potassium influx from the endolymph.":["u209"],"copy|The resulting receptor potential triggers glutamate release onto the auditory nerve.":["u210"],"copy|Mechanical amplification, transduction and neural signaling are therefore three places a sound may lose information (p492–493).":["u211"],"copy|Figure 34-4 compares surface preparations from mice aged 3 and 23 months.":["u212"],"copy|Orderly rows of outer hair cells in the younger animal become irregular, with gaps in the older animal.":["u213"],"copy|In mammals, lost sensory cells ordinarily leave scars rather than functional replacements.":["u214"],"copy|Loss concentrated in the basal cochlea disproportionately affects high frequencies.":["u215"],"copy|The photograph demonstrates a structural change in an animal preparation.":["u216"],"copy|It cannot identify which cellular lesion is responsible for an individual older patient's audiogram or establish a human age equivalent for the animal findings (p493–495).":["u217"],"copy|Tables 34-1 and 34-2 answer different questions.":["u218"],"copy|Sound level in decibels describes how intense a stimulus is.":["u219"],"copy|Frequency in hertz describes its rate of vibration and relates to pitch.":["u220"],"copy|A whisper is roughly 35 dB, and ordinary conversation about 50–60 dB in Table 34-1.":["u221"],"copy|Increasing the level of a voice does not restore the consonant information if the listener's high-frequency processing remains poor.":["u222"],"copy|From the same table, a vacuum cleaner produces about 60–70 dB while a lawn mower or chain saw is around 105–110 dB.":["u223"],"copy|Occupational or recreational noise exposure depends on duration as well as peak intensity; the table contrasts 85 dB for an 8-hour workday with 100 dB for 15 minutes (p494).":["u224"],"copy|Table 34-2 places spoken vowels around 500–750 Hz, the /sh/ group at 1000–2000 Hz, and /f/, /k/, /s/, /t/ and /th/ at 3000–8000 Hz.":["u225"],"copy|Fundamental frequencies and their overtones together give speech and music their distinctive character.":["u226"],"copy|A listener with high-frequency loss can hear syllable timing and much of a speaker's voice through lower-frequency cues yet confuse words with similar vowels.":["u227"],"copy|In the Hebrew exam example, the two high-frequency consonants in the keyed word make it particularly vulnerable.":["u228"],"copy|The competing sounds in a busy room can remove contextual cues that made the same word intelligible one to one.":["u229"],"copy|Clarify the word or phrase in a quieter setting and face the listener so facial information can supplement the acoustic signal (p494–495).":["u230"],"copy|In Figure 34-5A an audiogram from a man aged 62 slopes downward in both ears.":["u231"],"copy|The downward direction means a more intense stimulus was required for detection.":["u232"],"copy|Responses around 250–500 Hz are comparatively preserved; the high-frequency thresholds reach levels at which ordinary conversation cannot supply the necessary cues.":["u233"],"copy|Panel B, from a man aged 64, is much flatter: thresholds are elevated over a broad range.":["u234"],"copy|The book associates this pattern with loss of metabolic support, including the endocochlear potential.":["u235"],"copy|Panel C, from a man aged 32, shows an approximately 45-dB notch at 4 kHz on the left, while the right is near normal.":["u236"],"copy|The focal asymmetry suggests noise exposure such as target shooting.":["u237"],"copy|These are worked patterns, not a rule that every sloping loss is caused exclusively by hair-cell loss or every flat loss by strial disease (p495–496).":["u238"],"copy|Start with the patient's actual communication difficulty.":["u239"],"copy|Someone may report that others mumble, respond to the wrong word at dinner, or stop attending gatherings long before saying that sounds are too quiet.":["u240"],"copy|Ask about time course, differences between ears, noise and drug exposures, and effects on everyday life.":["u241"],"copy|Examine the canal and tympanic membrane.":["u242"],"copy|Wax or a middle-ear problem can produce a conductive component, while gradual bilateral impairment is more compatible with age-related sensorineural loss.":["u243"],"copy|The family history and a screening inventory can uncover a problem; neither finding localizes the lesion.":["u244"],"copy|The book notes that even a result described as normal for age may leave substantial disability that merits rehabilitation (p489–493, p495).":["u245"],"copy|Use the test that addresses the patient's difficulty.":["u246"],"copy|Pure-tone testing measures the least intense sound detectable at selected frequencies in each ear.":["u247"],"copy|Tympanometry and reflex testing address middle-ear function and reflex pathways.":["u248"],"copy|Otoacoustic emissions give information about outer hair cells, whereas an auditory brainstem response assesses neural transmission.":["u249"],"copy|Word-recognition testing adds the demand to identify a spoken signal, and a speech-in-noise task measures performance against competing sound.":["u250"],"copy|These tests can diverge without any inconsistency in the patient's account.":["u251"],"copy|A person who hears a pure tone reliably may still require a much larger speech-to-noise advantage to extract words (p493, p495–497).":["u252"],"copy|Central processing adds a time dimension.":["u253"],"copy|Following rapid or compressed speech requires the listener to separate brief sounds, connect them to words and retain enough context to interpret the next phrase.":["u254"],"copy|The chapter links performance on some complex listening tasks with working memory.":["u255"],"copy|Slowing delivery and reducing noise can improve immediate communication without reversing a cochlear lesion.":["u256"],"copy|When evaluating possible cognitive impairment, make sure the instructions were heard; otherwise the assessment may partly measure access to the test.":["u257"],"copy|A hearing-adapted cognitive assessment can help when standard spoken instructions disadvantage the patient (p496–497).":["u258"],"copy|Heritability estimates concern variation within the population examined.":["u259"],"copy|The Framingham comparison of relatives with spouses supports a genetic contribution but does not assign a percentage of one patient's hearing loss to genes.":["u260"],"copy|Exposure to noise and ototoxic agents accumulates across the same years.":["u261"],"copy|Diabetes can affect cochlear vascular supply and central auditory processing; cardiovascular disease may compromise the highly vascular stria.":["u262"],"copy|The energy requirements for maintaining the endocochlear potential make a vascular contribution plausible.":["u263"],"copy|Most human evidence is observational, however, and common risk factors tend to cluster.":["u264"],"copy|Ask for a timeline of changes and exposures alongside family history, rather than assigning the loss to one diagnosis by default (p490–491, p498).":["u265"],"copy|Different animal models reproduce different components.":["u266"],"copy|The gerbil can model a strial pattern with lower endocochlear potential, reduced blood flow and metabolic changes.":["u267"],"copy|Mouse strains vary greatly in the age at which sensory loss begins.":["u268"],"copy|Noise may injure connections between hair cells and nerve fibers even when the temporary increase in pure-tone thresholds recovers.":["u269"],"copy|Findings in one species, strain or exposure setting do not settle whether a treatment modifies human presbycusis.":["u270"],"copy|In the aging cochlea, altered oxidative defenses, mitochondrial DNA damage, vascular change and altered synapses can interact.":["u271"],"copy|Evidence of oxidative damage in human tissue is evidence of association, not proof that supplementing an antioxidant will preserve hearing (p496–499).":["u272"],"copy|Prevention studies illustrate the distinction.":["u273"],"copy|Some supplemented animal groups had better outcomes, but study size, strain, age at treatment and even kennel noise complicated interpretation.":["u274"],"copy|In one long-term mouse comparison an antioxidant-enriched diet did not improve age-related hearing loss.":["u275"],"copy|A Dutch trial found a small low-frequency benefit after 3 years of folic acid, while the high-frequency losses most characteristic of presbycusis continued.":["u276"],"copy|That result does not establish broad clinical protection.":["u277"],"copy|A history of harmful sound levels or potentially ototoxic drugs is still actionable: identify exposures that can reasonably be reduced and reassess function over time (p490–491, p498–499).":["u278"],"copy|Figure 34-6 plots aided and unaided thresholds for the same listener.":["u279"],"copy|The open-circle curve worsens at progressively higher frequencies.":["u280"],"copy|The filled-circle curve with amplification approaches normal through about 3000 Hz in that example; there is little reason to boost a low-frequency region already functioning well.":["u281"],"copy|Nor does the aid restore all the highest frequencies, where surviving sensory capacity may be too limited.":["u282"],"copy|Fitting a digital aid to the audiogram is the beginning of rehabilitation.":["u283"],"copy|Return to the situations the person named at the start: understanding a spouse across the table, following several voices, answering a telephone or recognizing an alarm.":["u284"],"copy|Lack of use may reflect a mismatch between those needs and the device as fitted rather than absence of disability (p499–500).":["u285"],"copy|When well-fitted aids still leave speech unintelligible, ask how much additional acoustic gain could accomplish.":["u286"],"copy|A cochlear implant converts incoming sound into a pattern of electrical stimulation delivered through a cochlear electrode to the auditory nerve.":["u287"],"copy|The result depends partly on the surviving nerve and on learning to interpret this input.":["u288"],"copy|Many recipients can understand speech better and use the telephone, while unfamiliar contexts and music can remain difficult.":["u289"],"copy|The percentage of sentences understood through the poorer ear and the decibel threshold averaged across tones are different measurements; each belongs with the specific book or article criterion cited above.":["u290"],"copy|The appropriate answer to the article-based exam question is referral for a candidacy evaluation, not a guarantee of implantation or a precise postoperative score (p499–500).":["u291"],"copy|Improved hearing has immediate value for participation, confidence and communication with clinicians regardless of its possible effect on long-term cognition.":["u292"],"copy|Observational studies can associate hearing loss with cognitive decline and h